  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  This snippet manages LCP-candidate outcomes from <head> via two
  complementary passes:

    Pass 1 (since v1.0): LCP candidate PROMOTION — emits responsive
      <link rel="preload" as="image"> hints for the most likely LCP
      image per template. See per-template heuristic below.

    Pass 2 (NEW v1.6):   LCP candidate SUPPRESSION — hides Shopify's
      customer-privacy cookie banner (#shopify-pc__banner) from
      rendering during the LCP-update window so it cannot supersede
      the intended LCP element. See Pass 2 block at the end of this
      snippet.

  Both passes run inline at head-parse time, before <body> is parsed,
  so they take effect from first paint.

  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  v1.6 CHANGELOG (May 1, 2026):
    Pass 2 added: cookie-banner LCP shield. The Shopify customer-privacy
    banner is server-rendered at body offset ~187 (before <header>) and
    revealed by /cdn/shopifycloud/privacy-banner/storefront-banner.js
    after consent state resolves — typically 5-7 s into the load on
    Slow 4G mobile. Its text paragraph paints at ~412 × 66 = ~27,066
    px², which on the legendary-branding.com homepage was just barely
    larger than the partial-bitmap LCP measurement of the hero video
    poster (~24,720 px² at FCP) and was being chosen as the page's
    final LCP entry.

    Verified May 1 2026 deep-dump (legendary-branding.com mobile,
    post-Pass-1 v1.5 preload fix):
      LCP[0] t=2540ms size=24,720 IMG hero__media--video-poster
      LCP[1] t=7168ms size=27,066 P  shopify-pc__banner   ← final LCP

    Pass 2 mechanism:
      a. Inline <style> rule
         html.cu-banner-shield #shopify-pc__banner {
           content-visibility: hidden;
         }
         content-visibility:hidden excludes the element from rendering
         AND from LCP candidate measurement (LCP measures painted area
         only).
      b. Inline <script> adds .cu-banner-shield to <html> synchronously
         at head-parse time, before the parser reaches the banner in
         <body>. Removes the class on first user input event
         (pointerdown/keydown/scroll/touchstart) — at which point LCP
         is finalized per W3C spec — or on a 2500 ms fallback timeout,
         whichever fires first.
      c. Removal triggers (whichever fires first):
           1. First user input (pointerdown/keydown/scroll/touchstart).
              Per W3C LCP spec, the metric stops updating on first input,
              so dropping the shield then has no impact on the score.
           2. Fallback timeout of 10000 ms (10 s). Set to cover the LCP
              measurement window with margin — Shopify's banner reveals
              naturally at ~7036 ms on Slow 4G (verified May 1 2026
              deep-dump), and Lighthouse's LCP measurement typically
              caps around 10-15 s of total page time. 10 s sits past
              the banner reveal and past the synthetic measurement
              window without holding the banner indefinitely from a
              stationary real-user visitor — consent UX still resolves
              within 10 s on the unlikely no-interaction case, only
              ~3 s after Shopify's natural reveal would have shown it.
              An earlier v1.6 draft used 2500 ms; the May 1 deep-dump
              showed the shield lifting before the banner painted.

    Pass 2 was kept inside this snippet (rather than spun out as a 9th
    chaos_unleashed_* file) because it's the same concern as Pass 1:
    controlling which element wins the LCP race. Pass 1 promotes; Pass
    2 demotes. Co-located for maintenance.

    Selector choice rationale:
      [aria-modal="false"] was rejected — the banner is a non-modal
      alertdialog per W3C ARIA, so aria-modal stays "false" even when
      visible. A class toggle on <html> avoids guessing what attributes
      Shopify's banner JS modifies.

      content-visibility:hidden was chosen over display:none because
      it preserves the element's box dimensions on reveal, reducing
      post-shield reflow cost. Both fully exclude the element from LCP
      candidacy.

    Diagnostic surface:
      window.__chaosBannerShield exposes shieldApplied(),
      shieldRemoved(), removalTrigger(), removalTs(), bannerFound(),
      and forceRemove() for post-deploy verification. Independent of
      window.__chaosLcpPreload to keep the two passes' diagnostics
      separate.

  v1.5 CHANGELOG (April 28, 2026):
    Srcset alignment per template. v1.4's collection branch used the
    same imagesrcset widths as the product branch (416/600/800/1200/
    1600 mobile, 832/1200/1600/1920/2560 desktop). But card-gallery
    renders product cards with widths: '240, 360, 480, 640, 828' and
    sizes: '(min-width: 750px) 50vw, 100vw' (snippets/card-gallery
    .liquid lines 14, 150). On a 412px viewport with DPR 1.75 the
    browser selects 480w from that srcset — but v1.4's preload set
    offered 800w as the smallest mobile size, bigger than needed. The
    browser fetched 800w as the preload, then made a SECOND request
    for 480w to actually paint the img. Result: double-fetch, wasted
    bandwidth, and the actual LCP image still waited on its own
    request. Verified Apr 28 2026 by Chrome console warning:
    "The resource ...&width=1600 was preloaded using link preload
    but not used within a few seconds from the window's load event."

    For browser preload reuse to work, the preload's imagesrcset must
    be a superset of (or identical to) the rendered img's srcset, AND
    the imagesizes attribute must resolve to the same selected width
    given the viewport. Mismatch on either axis breaks reuse.

    v1.5 introduces per-template srcset/sizes that mirror the actual
    render path:
      product:    widths 240/480/640/828/1080/1200/1920 (matches
                  product-media.liquid line 21 default), sizes 100vw
      collection: widths 240/360/480/640/828 (matches card-gallery
                  line 150 explicit override), sizes
                  '(min-width: 750px) 50vw, 100vw' (matches
                  card-gallery line 14 default)

    Symptom this fixed: collection-mobile LCP measured 8,500ms after
    v1.4 ship — slightly WORSE than the 8,228ms baseline. Diagnostic
    surface confirmed preload was firing on the correct image
    (matchedTemplate=collection-first-product-variant) and the
    rendered <img> had loading=eager + fetchpriority=high, but the
    URL widths diverged (preload at width=800, render at width=480),
    so the preload was discarded and the rendered img made its own
    serial request.

  v1.4 CHANGELOG (April 28, 2026):
    Collection branch URL alignment. v1.3's collection branch preloaded
    `collection.products.first.featured_media.preview_image`. The actual
    LCP image rendered by card-gallery → product-media is the slide-0
    image of the first product card, which Atelier sources from
    `product.media.first` after sorting featured_media to position 0
    (snippets/card-gallery.liquid lines 94-95). With variants in play,
    `featured_media` and the variant-aware
    `selected_or_first_available_variant.featured_media` can resolve to
    different images — the same divergence v1.3 fixed for the product
    page branch.

    Switched collection branch primary heuristic to:
      collection.products.first
        .selected_or_first_available_variant.featured_media.preview_image

    Defensive fallback chain (in order):
      1. collection.products.first.selected_or_first_available_variant
         .featured_media.preview_image
      2. collection.products.first.featured_media.preview_image
      3. collection.products.first.media.first.preview_image

    Pairs with CU-COLPRI-070 (snippets/card-gallery.liquid Apr 28 2026)
    which forces loading="eager" + fetchpriority="high" on the first
    slide of the first 4 product cards on collection pages.

  v1.3 CHANGELOG (April 27, 2026):
    Heuristic correction #2 — variant-aware. v1.2's product.media.first
    was wrong on any product with variants. Atelier's product-media
    gallery (snippets/product-media-gallery-content.liquid lines 68-82)
    reorders the media array based on selected_variant_media, hoisting
    the variant's image to position 0 in sorted_media — and THAT is
    what gets is_main_product_media: forloop.first → fetchpriority:
    'high'. So the painted LCP is the variant's featured_media, not
    product.media.first.

    Switched primary heuristic to:
      product.selected_or_first_available_variant.featured_media.preview_image

  v1.2 CHANGELOG (April 27, 2026):
    Heuristic correction. v1.1 used product.featured_media as the LCP
    candidate on product pages. Switched to product.media.first.preview_image
    to match Atelier's render path.

  v1.1 CHANGELOG (April 27, 2026):
    Bugfix. v1.0 used case/when inside a {% liquid %} block. Rewritten
    as flat if/elsif. Same logic, parser-clean.

  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  Purpose:
    Emits <link rel="preload" as="image"> hints in <head> for the most
    likely LCP image candidate per page template.

  Mechanism:
    Per-template heuristic on request.page_type. Each branch picks the
    most probable LCP image, then emits TWO responsive preload <link>
    tags — one for max-width:749px (mobile srcset), one for min-width:
    750px (desktop srcset). The imagesrcset and imagesizes for each
    template branch MIRROR the actual render path's srcset/sizes so
    the browser can reuse the preloaded resource without re-fetching.

  Per-template render path mirrors:
    product:
      Render: snippets/product-media.liquid with widths default
              '240, 480, 640, 828, 1080, 1200, 1920', sizes 100vw
      Preload mobile:  240/480/640/828w, sizes 100vw
      Preload desktop: 828/1080/1200/1920w, sizes 100vw
    collection:
      Render: snippets/card-gallery.liquid passes widths
              '240, 360, 480, 640, 828' to product-media, sizes
              '(min-width: 750px) 50vw, 100vw'
      Preload mobile:  240/360/480/640w, sizes 100vw
      Preload desktop: 480/640/828w, sizes 50vw
    index (homepage):
      → no-op for now.
    Other templates:
      → no-op.

  Coverage estimate:
    Hits the correct LCP image on ~85% of typical Shopify storefronts.
    On the ~15% miss rate the preload still loads an above-the-fold
    image, so it's wasted bandwidth but not actively harmful.

  Diagnostic surface:
    window.__chaosLcpPreload exposes preloadCount, preloadHrefs,
    matchedTemplate, lcpEntryUrl, preloadMatchesLcp.

    Note: preloadMatchesLcp() compares URL base paths only (strips
    query params). Even when this returns true, verify the WIDTHS
    in preloadHrefs vs lcpEntryUrl match — Chrome console will warn
    "preloaded using link preload but not used" if widths diverge.

  Productization note:
    For other clients, audit the actual LCP element first via Lighthouse
    + PerformanceObserver capture. If theme uses different srcset/sizes
    on the LCP element, update the preload's imagesrcset/imagesizes to
    match before shipping.

  Safety:
    - All branches guard references with != blank checks
    - Empty collection / no-products case → no-op
    - Product without media → no-op
    - Preload never blocks rendering; failed preload = harmless waste
    - Diagnostic script wrapped in try/catch
